chapter_notes.1B:
Material exposure is clear: Putin had direct presidential authority over nuclear doctrine, war decisions, and public nuclear signaling in 2022. The local structured prior had no 1B facts, so this pass is narrative-only.

**Reused Evidence**
None.

**New Evidence**
- Inherited baseline: Putin's 2020 nuclear-deterrence decree stayed in force during 2022 and made the president the overall authority for nuclear deterrence policy and use conditions.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Executive Order Approving the Basic Principles of State Policy of the Russian Federation on Nuclear Deterrence","publisher":"Official Internet Portal of Legal Information","publication_date":"2020-06-02","url":"https://publication.pravo.gov.ru/document/view/0001202006020040","claim":"Putin approved Russia's nuclear-deterrence doctrine, which remained the governing baseline in 2022 and made the president the overall authority for nuclear deterrence policy and use conditions.","locator":"official publication text and Kremlin summary page","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official legal publication of the presidential decree; the text is the binding baseline in force during 2022.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline in force throughout 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential act setting the formal nuclear decision framework before 2022","contrary_evidence":["The document is a formal baseline, not proof of prudent 2022 conduct.","Later 2022 actions moved the posture in a more dangerous direction."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.7","1B.10"]}
- Early-2022 restraint baseline, but only at the level of joint state language, not proof of later restraint.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement attributable to the Spokesperson for the Secretary-General on the Joint Statement of the Leaders of the Five Nuclear-Weapon States on Preventing Nuclear War and Avoiding Arms Races","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-01-03","url":"https://www.un.org/sg/en/content/sg/statements/2022-01-03/statement-attributable-the-spokesperson-for-the-secretary-general-the-joint-statement-of-the-leaders-of-the-five-nuclear-weapon-states-preventing-nuclear-war-and","claim":"The UN Secretary-General welcomed the five-nuclear-weapon-state statement that pledged prevention of nuclear war and avoidance of arms races, a useful early-2022 restraint baseline for Russia.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN Secretariat statement directly identifies the joint declaration and its nuclear-risk framing.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Early-2022 baseline before the full-scale invasion and later escalations","ruler_attribution":"State-level joint declaration that included Russia under Putin","contrary_evidence":["A joint statement does not show later compliance in 2022.","Russia's later war conduct undercut the implied restraint."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4"]}
- Pre-invasion nuclear justification rhetoric.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Путин заявил, что угроза Украины создать собственное ядерное оружие не пустая бравада","publisher":"TASS","publication_date":"2022-02-21","url":"https://tass.ru/politika/13791761","claim":"On the eve of invasion, Putin publicly argued that a Ukrainian nuclear weapons program would be a real danger and an answerable threat, using it to justify heightened security claims.","locator":"headline and paragraphs 79-82","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Russian state wire reproduces Putin's televised remarks with direct quotations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate pre-invasion nuclear rhetoric in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin","contrary_evidence":["The claim about a Ukrainian nuclear program was speculative and not established.","It was used as a justification for escalation rather than risk reduction."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.5"]}
- Full-scale invasion plus veiled nuclear warning.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's Putin authorises special military operation against Ukraine","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-02-24","url":"https://m.investing.com/news/world-news/russias-putin-authorises-special-military-operation-against-ukraine-2770751?ampMode=1","claim":"Putin launched the full-scale invasion of Ukraine and paired it with a veiled warning that interference could bring consequences Russia had never shown before.","locator":"opening paragraphs and Putin quote about consequences","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire report on the opening of the war, quoting the president directly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Start of the 2022 war","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin","contrary_evidence":["Kremlin framing claimed the operation was defensive and limited.","That framing did not reduce the escalation effect of the launch and warning."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.5","1B.8"]}
- Nuclear alert order during the first week of war.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin orders putting deterrence forces on special alert in wake of NATO's aggressive statements against Russia","publisher":"Interfax","publication_date":"2022-02-27","url":"https://interfax.com/newsroom/top-stories/74601/","claim":"Putin ordered Russia's deterrence forces onto a special alert status, explicitly linking the move to NATO rhetoric and sanctions during the Ukraine invasion.","locator":"lead paragraph and quoted instruction to Shoigu and Gerasimov","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous report with the president's quoted instruction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Early-war nuclear alert escalation in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal order from Putin to the defense leadership","contrary_evidence":["Russia portrayed the move as defensive deterrence.","Independent observers treated it as dangerous nuclear brinkmanship."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.3","1B.8"]}
- Arms-control setback in August 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia suspends inspections stipulated in New START arms control treaty","publisher":"TASS","publication_date":"2022-08-09","url":"https://tass.com/politics/1491611/amp","claim":"Russia temporarily suspended New START inspections, saying the U.S. inspection plan and travel restrictions made the regime look like a provocation and denied parity.","locator":"lead paragraphs quoting the Foreign Ministry and Ryabkov","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous state-wire report on an official arms-control suspension decision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 arms-control deterioration","ruler_attribution":"State decision under Putin's presidency; not a private personal remark but attributable to his government","contrary_evidence":["Moscow said it remained committed to the treaty's provisions.","Washington said the suspension was irresponsible and destabilizing."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.8","1B.10"]}
- Alliance-proliferation step with Belarus.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin promises Belarus nuclear-capable missiles to counter aggressive West","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-06-25","url":"https://www.tbsnews.net/world/putin-promises-belarus-nuclear-capable-missiles-counter-aggressive-west-447258","claim":"Putin said Russia would supply Belarus with Iskander-M missiles capable of carrying nuclear warheads and could also upgrade Belarusian aircraft for that role.","locator":"report body and Putin-Lukashenko meeting summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on a direct bilateral meeting and a specific capability transfer announcement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 proliferation and alliance-risk escalation","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal decision/announcement by Putin","contrary_evidence":["Putin and Lukashenko framed the move as symmetrical deterrence, not proliferation.","The practical effect was to deepen nuclear exposure in Belarus."],"lenses":["1B.6","1B.5","1B.10"]}
- Zaporizhzhia safety crisis, with Russian forces in control.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Update 94 - IAEA Director General Statement on Situation in Ukraine","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.iaea.org/newscenter/pressreleases/update-94-iaea-director-general-statement-on-situation-in-ukraine","claim":"The IAEA reported that the Zaporizhzhia nuclear plant had lost external power lines during the conflict and said it was controlled by Russian forces while staffed by Ukrainians, underscoring unsafe wartime nuclear conditions.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IAEA safety bulletin from the period documenting site control and power-line loss.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 wartime nuclear-safety crisis","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but material attribution through Russian forces operating under Putin's war","contrary_evidence":["IAEA said safety systems remained operational at the time.","The report still described an unstable and dangerous environment created by the conflict."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8","1B.9","1B.10"]}
- Later Zaporizhzhia escalation, with explosions at the plant.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Update 128 - IAEA Director General Statement on Situation in Ukraine","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.iaea.org/newscenter/pressreleases/update-128-iaea-director-general-statement-on-situation-in-ukraine","claim":"The IAEA said explosions hit the Zaporizhzhia plant area and reiterated that a nuclear safety and security zone was urgently needed because the site was in a volatile combat environment.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IAEA incident report describing damage and escalation at the plant.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 nuclear-safety deterioration","ruler_attribution":"Indirect attribution through Russian control of the territory and wartime conduct","contrary_evidence":["The IAEA did not assign full blame in the text excerpt.","The broader implication remained that Russian wartime control made the site unsafe."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8","1B.9","1B.10"]}
- Narrow diplomatic channel on Zaporizhzhia remained open in late September.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IAEA Proposal for Ukraine Nuclear Safety and Security Protection Zone Wins Support as Talks Begin on Its Establishment","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency","publication_date":"2022-09-22","url":"https://nucleus-qa.iaea.org/sites/connect/_layouts/15/listform.aspx?ContentTypeID=0x010098D54B64E39A354DB05F7672AB6C615E&ID=407&ListId=%7B76F64203-9B4B-4C8A-862E-8B393FEE1E6D%7D&PageType=4","claim":"By late September, the IAEA said talks had begun with Ukraine and Russia on a nuclear safety and security protection zone around Zaporizhzhia, showing a narrow diplomatic channel remained open.","locator":"ConnectNews item metadata and title line","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"IAEA internal news item records the date and the negotiations it was running.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Late-2022 crisis diplomacy","ruler_attribution":"Indirect attribution; shows a channel involving Russia under Putin rather than a specific Putin initiative","contrary_evidence":["The talks were a response to a dangerous situation, not evidence of restraint.","The IAEA noted the zone was still only being negotiated."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.4"]}
- September mobilization address with explicit nuclear threshold language.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address by the President of the Russian Federation","publisher":"President of Russia","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://special.kremlin.ru/catalog/countries/UA/events/69390","claim":"In the mobilization address, Putin tied Russia's defense of territorial integrity to using all available means and explicitly rejected the idea that the warning was bluffing.","locator":"quoted passage in the speech transcript","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Kremlin transcript of a live presidential address with direct nuclear-threshold language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Peak escalation in September 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin","contrary_evidence":["The Kremlin claimed the warning was defensive.","The speech expanded the perceived nuclear risk rather than reducing it."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.5","1B.8"]}
- Annexation rhetoric invoked Hiroshima and Nagasaki as precedent.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Путин заявил, что США единственные в мире дважды применили ядерное оружие","publisher":"TASS","publication_date":"2022-09-30","url":"https://tass.ru/politika/15921155","claim":"At the annexation ceremony, Putin invoked Hiroshima and Nagasaki as a precedent and used that argument to justify the annexation speech's broader confrontation framing.","locator":"headline and paragraphs 23-28","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct state-wire report on a live presidential speech with specific nuclear rhetoric.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-September annexation and nuclear-rhetoric escalation","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin","contrary_evidence":["The comparison was rhetorical, not an operational instruction.","It normalized nuclear precedent language in a crisis context."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.5"]}
- Annexation immediately converted occupied territory into a nuclear shield claim.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Kremlin says any attack on annexed territory will be an attack on Russia","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-10-01","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/01/ukraine-crisis-annexations-russia-kremlin","claim":"The Kremlin said attacks on the annexed Ukrainian regions would be treated as attacks on Russia, while Reuters noted Putin had just threatened nuclear use to defend territorial integrity.","locator":"lines around the Peskov quote and annexation summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report relayed by Euronews, with a specific attributable Kremlin quote and immediate nuclear implication.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate annexation aftermath in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct state attribution tied to Putin's annexation move and prior nuclear threat","contrary_evidence":["Russia claimed this was a legal consequence of annexation.","International actors treated it as coercive nuclear shielding of conventional war."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8","1B.10"]}
- Multilateral condemnation of Russia's dangerous nuclear rhetoric and Zaporizhzhia risk.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Non-Proliferation Treaty Review Conference Ends without Adopting Substantive Outcome Document Due to Opposition by One Member State","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/dc3850.doc.htm","claim":"The NPT review conference ended without a final document and many delegates condemned the Russian Federation's dangerous nuclear rhetoric and the risks around Zaporizhzhia.","locator":"opening paragraphs and delegate interventions in the meeting coverage","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN meeting coverage summarizing multilateral criticism of Russian nuclear behavior and plant risk.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 multilateral nuclear-risk assessment","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Russian Federation under Putin's war leadership","contrary_evidence":["Russia argued the meeting still had valuable discussions and blamed others for the failure.","The dominant multilateral reading was that Russian conduct was the source of the risk."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.4","1B.5","1B.8","1B.9"]}
- General Assembly scrutiny of the annexation step.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"General Assembly Takes Up Draft Resolution Condemning Russian Federation's Annexation of Several Territories in Eastern Ukraine, Resuming Emergency Special Session","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12456.doc.htm","claim":"The General Assembly took up a draft resolution condemning Russia's annexation of occupied Ukrainian territories, reflecting broad international rejection of the coercive annexation step linked to Putin's nuclear-truncated territorial logic.","locator":"opening summary paragraph","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN coverage of the emergency special session and the annexation condemnation item.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"October 2022 annexation response","ruler_attribution":"Indirect attribution to the Russian Federation and Putin's annexation decision","contrary_evidence":["This is a political process document, not a court ruling.","Still, it shows the diplomatic environment around Putin's escalation."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8","1B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I considered roughly 30-plus documents across Kremlin transcripts, TASS, Interfax, Reuters syndications, IAEA incident reports, UN meeting coverage, and a small number of secondary analysis pieces.
- I rejected several near-duplicates of the same events, especially multiple English rewrites of the Feb. 24 invasion speech, the Feb. 27 alert order, and the Sept. 21 nuclear threat.
- I rejected out-of-period material, especially 2023 tactical-nuclear-in-Belarus follow-ups, 2024 doctrine revisions, and 2025-2026 retrospectives.
- I rejected commentary that repeated the same facts without adding a distinct claim or locator.
- I rejected some blocked or awkward-to-open pages when a search snippet already gave the needed claim and the source was otherwise redundant.
- I kept the set to 15 URLs because they cover distinct decision points and counterpoints without padding the same event through many publishers.

**Remaining Gaps**
- There is still weak direct evidence for 1B.3, specifically whether Putin strengthened command-and-control discipline, custody, or safety procedures in 2022.
- There is still thin direct evidence on 1B.7, meaning whether he invested in expert nuclear-risk institutions rather than loyalists or ideologues.
- Public evidence for private crisis de-escalation is limited. The IAEA channel shows dialogue, but not clear presidential restraint.
- The record is strong on threat rhetoric, escalation, annexation shielding, and arms-control setback. It is weaker on internal safety management and off-camera decision making.
- More search would likely help only for those gaps, not for the already well-supported danger/rhetoric lenses.

chapter_notes.2B:
**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.8","2B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0015"],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.10"]}

**New evidence**
- Direct initiation of the full-scale war.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's Putin authorises 'special military operation' against Ukraine","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-02-24","url":"https://m.investing.com/news/world-news/russias-putin-authorises-special-military-operation-against-ukraine-2770751?ampMode=1","claim":"Putin authorized the full-scale invasion of Ukraine and said the goal was to demilitarise and 'denazify' Ukraine.","locator":"opening paragraphs and Putin quote","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report quoting the president directly on the launch decision and justification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"War onset in February 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin","contrary_evidence":["The Kremlin framed the operation as defensive.","The report shows the operational choice was still an invasion."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}
- Early negotiation window existed, but it was narrow and inconclusive.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Talk of compromise on both sides lifts hope for Russia-Ukraine breakthrough","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-17","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/17/uk-ukraine-crisis","claim":"Reuters reported that both Moscow and Kyiv were discussing compromise formulas, including Ukrainian neutrality outside NATO, and that proposals were nearing agreement.","locator":"lead paragraphs and quotes from Lavrov, Zelenskiy, and the Kremlin","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on the negotiations and the Russian position.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Early March 2022 peace-talk window","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; shows negotiators operating under Putin's war decision and Russian state position","contrary_evidence":["Ukraine still demanded a ceasefire and withdrawal of Russian troops.","The war continued while talks remained unresolved."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7"]}
- Russia publicly signaled a narrower war aim only after battlefield setbacks.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia reframes war goals as Ukrainians advance near Kyiv","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-26","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/26/uk-ukraine-crisis","claim":"Russia signaled it was scaling back ambitions in Ukraine to focus on Donbass, saying the first phase was mostly complete.","locator":"lead and quoted statement from the Russian General Staff","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on a major operational shift announced by Russian defense leadership.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late March 2022 battlefield and war-aim adjustment","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin's state and military leadership","contrary_evidence":["Western officials said the original aims were a pretext for overthrowing Ukraine's government.","The shift followed military failure near Kyiv rather than a peace settlement."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Russia made a concrete de-escalation move near Kyiv and Chernihiv after Istanbul talks.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia says it will sharply cut military activity near Kyiv, Chernihiv","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-30","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/30/uk-ukraine-crisis-turkey-talks-russia","claim":"Russia said it would sharply reduce military activity around Kyiv and Chernihiv to increase mutual trust and create conditions for further negotiations.","locator":"lead paragraph and quoted statement by Deputy Defence Minister Alexander Fomin","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on an announced operational reduction tied to negotiations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Post-Istanbul talks, March 2022","ruler_attribution":"Russian state action under Putin's authority, but the reduction was tactical rather than a verified peace breakthrough","contrary_evidence":["The reduction did not end the war.","Russian forces kept major offensives elsewhere."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Early civilian harm and likely humanitarian-law violations were already widespread by late March.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Update on the human rights situation in Ukraine, 24 February – 26 March 2022","publisher":"UN Human Rights Monitoring Mission in Ukraine","publication_date":"2022-03-28","url":"https://ukraine.ohchr.org/en/175994-update-human-rights-situation-ukraine-EN","claim":"The UN mission said Russian armed forces used explosive weapons with wide-area effects in populated areas and verified at least 2,909 civilian casualties by 26 March.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UN human-rights monitoring bulletin with verified casualty and conduct findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate invasion phase in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Russian armed forces operating under Putin's war command","contrary_evidence":["The UN noted some allegations also concerned Ukrainian forces in limited areas.","The dominant verified pattern was Russian conduct in populated areas."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}
- Indiscriminate attacks in Kharkiv.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine: 'Anyone can die at any time': Indiscriminate attacks by Russian forces in Kharkiv, Ukraine","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/eur50/5682/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty said Russian forces launched relentless indiscriminate bombardments against Kharkiv's residential neighborhoods, killing and injuring civilians and using widely banned cluster munitions.","locator":"report summary and opening section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed field investigation with digital evidence and on-the-ground testimony.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 battlefield conduct","ruler_attribution":"Russian forces under Putin's war leadership","contrary_evidence":["Russia denied unlawful attacks.","The report found the pattern incompatible with civilian protection duties."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}
- Mariupol theatre strike.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine: Deadly Mariupol theatre strike 'a clear war crime' by Russian forces – new investigation","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/06/ukraine-deadly-mariupol-theatre-strike-a-clear-war-crime-by-russian-forces-new-investigation/","claim":"Amnesty concluded that Russian military forces likely deliberately targeted the Mariupol drama theatre despite knowing civilians were sheltering there.","locator":"lead paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigation based on survivor interviews and digital evidence, with a clear conclusion on the strike.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Spring 2022 siege warfare","ruler_attribution":"Russian military forces under Putin's command","contrary_evidence":["Russia denied bombing the theatre.","The evidence presented by Amnesty pointed the other way."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}
- Grain exports were partly rescued by a deal, showing a limited humanitarian opening despite the war.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ukraine and Russia seal grain deal as U.S. pledges more military aid","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-07-21","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/commodities-news/ukraines-ports-to-reopen-under-deal-to-be-signed-friday-turkey-says-2851164","claim":"Russia and Ukraine signed a landmark deal to unblock grain exports from Black Sea ports and ease an international food crisis.","locator":"lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters wire on a major negotiated humanitarian/economic arrangement.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"July 2022 negotiated humanitarian corridor","ruler_attribution":"Russian state participation under Putin, though the arrangement was multilateral and brokered by the UN and Turkey","contrary_evidence":["The deal did not end the broader war.","Subsequent strikes threatened implementation."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- Putin combined mobilization with explicit nuclear coercion and annexation backing.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address by the President of the Russian Federation","publisher":"President of Russia","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://special.kremlin.ru/catalog/countries/UA/events/69390","claim":"Putin announced partial mobilization, tied the war to defense of territorial integrity, and said Russia would use all available means, adding that the warning was not bluff.","locator":"speech transcript paragraphs containing the territorial-integrity and 'not bluff' language","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Kremlin transcript of a live presidential address with direct attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Peak escalation in September 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin","contrary_evidence":["The Kremlin said the warning was defensive.","The speech escalated the war and widened coercive pressure."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.8","2B.10"]}
- The mobilization/annexation move was a major escalation, not a peace step.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia triggers plan to formally annex occupied Ukrainian regions","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/09/21/ukraine-crisis","claim":"Russian-controlled regions announced referendums on joining Russia after battlefield setbacks, and Reuters described the move as a serious escalation in Moscow's standoff with the West.","locator":"lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report on the annexation trigger and escalation context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 escalation and referendum rollout","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Putin's state and proxy apparatus in occupied Ukrainian territory","contrary_evidence":["Russia framed the move as local self-determination.","The votes were widely denounced as coerced and illegitimate."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.5","2B.8","2B.10"]}
- Annexation was formalized through Russia's own legal machinery.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's annexation plan in Ukraine: Kremlin signing ceremony","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-30","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/09/30/ukraine-crisis-annexation","claim":"Reuters reported that Putin would host a Kremlin signing ceremony to incorporate four Ukrainian regions, a step toward formally annexing about 15 percent of Ukraine.","locator":"lead paragraphs and bullet summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters summary of the annexation legal step and Kremlin ceremony.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late September 2022 annexation","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal and state attribution to Putin's annexation decision","contrary_evidence":["Russia claimed the regions were joining by referendum.","International actors treated the annexation as illegal seizure."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.5","2B.10"]}
- Russian-language independent reporting on the ratification step.
