SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Law on monitoring activities of persons under foreign influence","publisher":"President of Russia","publication_date":"2022-07-14","url":"https://en.kremlin.ru/catalog/keywords/31/events/copy/68891","claim":"Putin signed the federal law on monitoring persons under foreign influence, consolidating foreign-agent provisions and defining the designation broadly enough to cover people receiving foreign support and engaging in political or information activity.","locator":"official summary text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Kremlin publication summarizing the signed law and its operative scope.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 legislative tightening","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin as the signing authority","contrary_evidence":["The Kremlin said the law improved legal regulation and transparency.","The law still materially widened tools for political exclusion and censorship."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- Russia ceased to be a party to the European Convention on Human Rights in September 2022, closing a major external remedy path.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia ceases to be party to the European Convention on Human Rights","publisher":"Council of Europe","publication_date":"2022-09-16","url":"https://www.coe.int/en/web/portal/-/russia-ceases-to-be-party-to-the-european-convention-on-human-rights","claim":"The Council of Europe said Russia ceased to be a party to the European Convention on Human Rights on 16 September 2022, ending a major external legal avenue for complaints and judgments.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Council of Europe notice on the legal cutoff date and its consequences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 accountability rollback","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Russian state under Putin, though caused by Russia's expulsion and withdrawal process","contrary_evidence":["The Court retained residual jurisdiction for earlier acts.","That residual jurisdiction did not restore the lost domestic remedy path."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- Putin's own 2020 constitutional reset allowed him to stay in power until 2036.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin approves changes allowing him to stay in power until 2036","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2020-03-10","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/russias-ruling-party-backs-idea-to-allow-putin-to-run-again-for-president-ria-2105790","claim":"Putin backed constitutional changes that reset his term count and allowed him to remain eligible to rule until 2036.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report on the constitutional reset and its effect on term limits.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline for the 2022 period","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin's constitutional redesign","contrary_evidence":["Putin publicly said he favored term limits in a politically mature system.","The practical effect was still to remove normal succession pressure."],"lenses":["4B.8","4B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across official Russian legal texts, CEC-related materials, Golos election-monitoring reports, EPDE, OVD-Info, Amnesty, Human Rights Watch, CPJ, Reuters wires, and Council of Europe notices.
- I rejected several duplicates of the same themes, especially repeated reports on the same March 2022 censorship package, the same September 2022 Novaya Gazeta closure, and the same foreign-agents law in different outlets.
- I rejected out-of-period material from 2021 on Memorial's shutdown as direct 2022 evidence, because 2022 sources already documented the continuing crackdown more cleanly.
- I rejected broad secondary explainers when they added no materially distinct claim beyond the primary legal or monitoring sources.
- I also rejected some later 2023-2026 stories about Putin challengers and regional elections because they were outside the target period and not needed to establish the 2022 pattern.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct 2022 evidence on internal court independence and elite contestation remains thin; the record shows erosion and remedy removal more clearly than it shows any positive institution-building.
- I did not find strong 2022 evidence of Putin respecting or strengthening succession rules. The best evidence is actually the inherited 2020 reset that made long-term continuation possible.
- There is limited ruler-specific evidence on whether Putin personally directed individual censorship or candidate exclusions, but the legal and administrative system is clearly attributable to his government.
- More search would probably refine attribution and mechanics, but it is unlikely to change the main chapter picture: 2022 further entrenched an already authoritarian system, especially through war-linked repression, media closure, and electoral narrowing.

